The design decisions that actually matter\n\n**Ceremony depth is the dominant confound, and it would have produced a false NO-GO.** PAEOS-9 §8.3 says Trace-A auto-discharges L03–L11. If B0.0/B0.1/B0.2 all ran Trace-A, then Option/TradeOff/Constraint/Component were never produced — so a naive probe would score them `absent` and report high loss for reasons that have nothing to do with A8's claim (prose-vs-typed). Neutralised three ways: a selection rule requiring a Trace-B goal if one exists, an `n/a-auto-discharged` exclusion evaluated *before* search, and a `N ≥ 87` sample-validity floor.\n\n**`partial` vs `absent` is the instrument, not a nicety.** A8 asks whether artifacts are \"too unstructured to extract losslessly.\" Prose-but-present is the loss S1's extractor exists to remove; genuinely-missing is loss only an emitter change fixes. The gate weights structural loss (`absent_ratio ≤ 0.25`) separately from the blended headline.\n\n**Thresholds are pre-registered and each derived from a cited fact, not chosen.** `0.25` ≈ \"less than two of seven §6.1b bundle types' worth missing\"; Decision's `0.15` = at most 2 of 18 cells, justified because the decision-graph schema already exists (PAEOS-9 §5) and global rule 11 makes `constitutional_basis` mandatory. Freezing them here matters because the stage that sets the bar is structurally unable to see the data.\n\n**`Outcome` is excluded from the denominator.** §4.3 calls it \"the only node type that may be created long after its subject,\" sourced from L17/L19. A sealed review cannot contain it; charging it as loss would manufacture a NO-GO.\n\n## Two things to flag\n\n**The acceptance gate has a representability defect.** `^loss_ratio: 0\\.[0-9]+$` admits `[0.00, 0.99]` and cannot express `1.00` — total extraction failure, the strongest possible falsification of A8, is unrepresentable by the gate commissioning the probe. §5.5 handles it honestly (unreachable when G5 holds; declared clamping with the exact value in the